"Make it live" is four different designs, and picking the wrong one is how a feature that needed a 30-second poll acquires a reconnect bug that takes a quarter to stabilise.

## The four

![Polling, long polling, SSE and WebSocket compared](/diagrams/sysdesign/realtime-transports.svg)

## Choosing

The decision is almost entirely about **direction** and **frequency**:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A["Does the client need to<br/>send messages too?"] -->|Yes| WS["WebSocket"]
  A -->|No| B["How stale can it be?"]
  B -->|"Seconds are fine"| P["Polling"]
  B -->|"Must be immediate"| SSE["SSE"]
  WS --> C["Really? Typing indicators,<br/>cursors, acks?"]
  C -->|"No, just reading"| SSE
```

That last loop matters. Most "we need WebSockets" requirements are one-directional on inspection, and SSE gives you the same latency with automatic reconnection, plain HTTP semantics, and no custom protocol.

## Polling — the default you should have to argue your way out of

```javascript
useQuery({
  queryKey: ['notifications'],
  queryFn: fetchNotifications,
  refetchInterval: 30_000,
  // Don't poll a tab nobody is looking at.
  refetchIntervalInBackground: false,
});
```

Boring, debuggable with the network tab, works through every proxy, and has no reconnect logic because there is no connection. Its cost is wasted round trips, which only matters at high client counts or short intervals.

## SSE — the underrated one

```javascript
const es = new EventSource('/api/stream');
es.addEventListener('post', (e) => applyPost(JSON.parse(e.data)));
// The browser reconnects on its own and replays Last-Event-ID.
es.onerror = () => setConnectionState('reconnecting');
```

What you get for free: automatic reconnection with backoff, event ids so the server can resume from where you were, and ordinary HTTP — so your auth, your proxies, and your CDN all just work.

What you give up: no client→server channel (use a normal POST), and on HTTP/1.1 it consumes one of the browser's ~6 connections per origin. On HTTP/2 that limit is gone.

## WebSocket — when the client genuinely talks back

```javascript
// The half that is actually the work:
function connect(attempt = 0) {
  const ws = new WebSocket(url);
  ws.onopen = () => { attempt = 0; resubscribe(); flushOutbox(); };
  ws.onclose = () => {
    // Exponential backoff with jitter — without jitter every client
    // reconnects at the same instant and takes the server down again.
    const wait = Math.min(30_000, 2 ** attempt * 1000) * (0.5 + Math.random());
    setTimeout(() => connect(attempt + 1), wait);
  };
  ws.onmessage = (e) => apply(JSON.parse(e.data));
}
```

The connection is the easy part. The design is everything around it:

| Concern | What you must decide |
| --- | --- |
| **Reconnect** | Exponential backoff **with jitter** — a thundering herd will re-kill a recovering server |
| **Resume** | Resume from the last message id, never from "now" — otherwise every reconnect silently drops messages |
| **Auth** | Tokens expire mid-connection; decide whether you re-auth in-band or reconnect |
| **Ordering** | Messages can arrive out of order after a reconnect; sequence them |
| **Backpressure** | A fast server and a slow client means an unbounded queue; drop or coalesce |
| **Fan-out** | Many tabs → a `SharedWorker` or leader election, not one socket per tab |
| **Fallback** | Corporate proxies still break WebSockets; degrade to polling |

> "The socket is a day. Reconnect, resume, auth refresh, ordering and the proxy fallback are the quarter. That's why I'd only pay it if the client genuinely sends messages."

## Batching — the part everyone forgets

At high frequency the transport stops being the bottleneck and rendering does. 200 messages per second against a 60 fps budget means you are three times over before React runs.

```javascript
// Coalesce into one render per frame.
let pending = new Map();
let scheduled = false;

function onMessage(msg) {
  pending.set(msg.id, msg);          // last write wins per entity
  if (scheduled) return;
  scheduled = true;
  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    applyBatch(pending);
    pending = new Map();
    scheduled = false;
  });
}
```

Two wins at once: at most one render per frame, and superseded updates for the same entity are collapsed instead of each causing a render.

## Always design the disconnected state

Whatever you choose, the UI must answer "am I live right now?"

- A visible connection indicator once reconnection has been failing for a few seconds
- Reads fall back to the last known cache rather than emptying the screen
- Writes queue locally and flush on reconnect, with idempotency keys so retries do not duplicate
- On resume, reconcile from a cursor — never assume you have everything

## Common mistakes

- **WebSocket by default.** You have bought a reconnect problem for a one-way feed.
- **Reconnect without jitter.** Every client reconnects simultaneously and re-kills the server.
- **Resuming from "now".** Messages during the outage are silently lost; the UI is wrong with no error.
- **No batching.** The transport is fine and the main thread is on fire.
- **Polling a hidden tab.** Wasted battery and wasted origin capacity.

## Key takeaways

- Direction decides it: one-way is SSE, two-way is WebSocket, tolerant of staleness is polling.
- SSE gives reconnection, resume and plain HTTP for free; WebSocket makes all three your problem.
- Backoff must have jitter; resume must use a cursor.
- Coalesce updates into `requestAnimationFrame` — at high frequency, rendering is the bottleneck.
- Design the disconnected state explicitly; it is part of the feature.

## Interview follow-ups

- **"What happens when the connection drops for two minutes?"** — Backoff with jitter, resume from the last event id, reconcile against the cache, and show the reader they were disconnected.
- **"1,000 messages a second?"** — The transport is fine. Coalesce per entity into one render per frame, and consider moving parsing to a worker.
- **"Why not WebSocket, it's more powerful?"** — Power you do not use is cost you still pay: reconnect, resume, auth refresh, ordering, proxy fallback.
